design/firmware-evidence/f5/broken_final/b2_motor_q1.md:898:- First charge of step 26 **[PROPOSED by assembly step 26]**: the charger runs at `CHARGE_CURRENT`, 15 mA, to 4.35 V; the jig reads GET_BATTERY until the state of charge reaches 50 percent (500 in `soc_percent_x10`), then goes to step 27. From an empty cell that takes about 0.61 to 1.10 hours across US 7 to 13 [CALC: 0.5 x 18.2 / 15 and 0.5 x 32.9 / 15]. The cell's maker may set another storage level (S12); the reading depends on the battery model (section 10.5, item 4).
design/firmware-evidence/f5/broken_final/b2_motor_q1.md:1003:26. The fuel-gauge battery model for the curved cells of 18.2 to 32.9 mAh, the accuracy of its state of charge at 4.1 mA with the motor's charge added, and the cell's discharge limit against the motor start of 175 mA (hardware issue H7). Who answers: the cell maker (S12), then the bench.
design/firmware-evidence/f5/broken_final/b2_motor_q1.md:1030:| H7 | The motor draws below 80 mA running and up to 175 mA at each start (architecture 17.16), now straight from the cell through U11 (17.22): 4.4 C running and 9.6 C at the start on the smallest cell, 18.2 mAh, and 2.4 C and 5.3 C on the largest, 32.9 mAh [CALC, section 3.1]. A start takes up to 228 mA from the cell with every other load on (17.22). No cell discharge limit and no protection-circuit trip current is on file. | open | the patterns are unchanged; B15 and B5 stop recording before the cell is low, but a trip of the protection circuit in a motor start would cut power in a pulse | cell maker (Grepow), through open item S12: maximum pulse current of the cell and its protection circuit |
