erted what F5 changes): the header says Version F5, follows A5 sections 17.11 to 17.23 and has the list "Changes from F4 to F5" with 8 items; the removed-name rule runs from that list to "How to read this" and now also bans the older power chip's name, the F3 battery range, the old microphone and datasheet names and the discharge-limit property; `CHARGE_CURRENT` is 15,000 µA in `current-microamp`, a multiple of 500 inside 4,000 to 100,000 and at most 1 C of the smallest cell of architecture 17.17, and T-CHARGE has a pass limit (in F4 both were BLOCKED on S12); the microphone mode range is read from the Knowles datasheet copy (F4 used fixed numbers of the older part), the microphone part name is in 4.1 and 8.5, and the gain is 0 dB, the register 0x28 (2 x 0 + 0x28), the header `gain_code` equal to it, and the stored full scale (94 dB SPL minus the sensitivity read from the Knowles copy) equal to the acoustic overload point read from the same copy; section 3.1 names U11, MOT_EN, VMOT and the 0.55 ms delay and never Q1, and architecture 17.22 names U11 (F4 checked Q1 and full duty on VDD_3V0); the battery minutes of 1.1 and the hours of 8.5 are recomputed from the smallest and largest cells of architecture 17.17 and the recording current 4.0 + 2 x (0.63 - 0.58) mA (F4 checked a single range against architecture 17.10); the ship section 8.4 names TASKENTERSHIPMODE, the VBUS removal and EVENTVBUSREMOVED and no timed hibernate; SHIP_SAMPLE keeps the factory flag in 9.3 and in 1.2; section 7 starts with the protocol sentence. New in F5: every "SIGNAL Pn.nn" pair and every chip pin of firmware.md is checked against the U1 rows of `netlist.csv`, and the power chip, microphone and motor nets this file names (GPIO0 on PMIC_INT, LSOUT1 on VDD_MIC, LSOUT2 on VDD_SD, U5 and U6 SELECT, U11 EN, OUT and IN, R61 and R62) against their netlist rows. Changed in F6: the cells per size are read from the measured table of architecture 17.26 (no longer 17.17); the compile limit of 8.1 must equal 1 C of the smallest cell, 17,600 µA; the header says Version F6 with sections 17.11 to 17.26 and the list "Changes from F5 to F6" has 4 items; and no line outside the change lists may name a value of the calculated cells (18.2, 24.3 or 32.9 mAh, or 18,200 µA) unless the word "before" stands within 60 characters of it. Rules it cannot check (timing values, protocol layouts, the meaning of text) are checked by review.
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:192:- **After:** the ring now runs on its own cell. It draws about 0.03 mA in STANDBY [ESTIMATE of `design/firmware.md:750`]: 25.3 days on the smallest cell of 18.2 mAh [CALC: 18.2 / 0.03 / 24; cell `design/architecture.md:619`]. The joints cannot be reached after step 17.
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:272:- **Check:** T-CHARGE shows the charger on; the battery voltage in the status record (`design/firmware.md:654`) rises. Charge to 50 percent before ship mode [PROPOSED: reason, a partly charged cell ages less in storage and still wakes the ring in the case; the cell maker may set another level, S12]. A full charge takes 1.2 to 2.2 hours over US 7 to 13 [CALC: 18.2 / 15 and 32.9 / 15; capacities `design/architecture.md:611`, `design/architecture.md:617`].
design/protocol/ring-link-extensions-draft.md:15:- The ring holds the DESIGN CAPACITY of its cell as a build constant per ring size: the low end of the range of architecture 17.17 for that size (US 7 18.2 mAh, US 8 19.3, US 9 20.4, US 10 21.5, US 11 22.6, US 12 23.6, US 13 24.7). From it the ring derives the threshold in percent: the next whole percent above RESERVE_MAH / design capacity. **US 7:** (0.05 x 18.2 + 4.5) / 18.2 = 5.41 / 18.2 = 29.73 %, so **30 %** (5.46 mAh; 4.55 mAh above B5 = 66.4 minutes at 4.111 mA). **US 13:** (0.05 x 24.7 + 4.5) / 24.7 = 5.735 / 24.7 = 23.22 %, so **24 %** (5.928 mAh; 4.693 mAh above B5 = 68.5 minutes). The other sizes: US 8 29 %, US 9 28 %, US 10 26 %, US 11 25 %, US 12 25 % (calc03.out gives all seven; every one leaves at least 65.9 minutes above B5).
design/protocol/ring-link-core.md:75:**The reserve** is 40 percent of the fuel gauge's state of charge, one constant for every ring size. It is sized for the smallest cell of the smallest size, US 7 at 18.2 mAh (architecture 17.17); larger cells only gain.
design/protocol/ring-link-protocol.md:69:- The ring holds the DESIGN CAPACITY of its cell as a build constant per ring size: the low end of the range of architecture 17.17 for that size (US 7 18.2 mAh, US 8 19.3, US 9 20.4, US 10 21.5, US 11 22.6, US 12 23.6, US 13 24.7). From it the ring derives the threshold in percent: the next whole percent above RESERVE_MAH / design capacity. **US 7:** (0.05 x 18.2 + 4.5) / 18.2 = 5.41 / 18.2 = 29.73 %, so **30 %** (5.46 mAh; 4.55 mAh above B5 = 66.4 minutes at 4.111 mA). **US 13:** (0.05 x 24.7 + 4.5) / 24.7 = 5.735 / 24.7 = 23.22 %, so **24 %** (5.928 mAh; 4.693 mAh above B5 = 68.5 minutes). The other sizes: US 8 29 %, US 9 28 %, US 10 26 %, US 11 25 %, US 12 25 % (calc03.out gives all seven; every one leaves at least 65.9 minutes above B5).
design/protocol/protocol-notes.md:13:**The guarantee, in one place.** The reserve is 40 percent of the gauge's state of charge for every ring size. Worst ring: US 7, 18.2 mAh (architecture 17.17), aged to 80 percent, 14.56 mAh; B5 at 5 percent; recording at 4.164 mA (4.0 estimate, plus 0.111 live audio, plus 0.053 for the 17.19 microphones): (0.40 - 0.05) x 14.56 / 4.164 = 73.4 minutes, margin 13.4 minutes, which absorbs a gauge reading high by 6.4 percentage points. Guaranteed on any ring whose cell holds at least 80 percent of its design capacity; below that the ring reports `battery_aged` and the guarantee is not claimed. TO MEASURE: the gauge's error, its behaviour on an aged cell and the 3.45 V under-load trip of B5, by bench test G1 (defined in the core, section 3). How the ring learns the capacity is TO FIND (open question 2).
design/protocol/protocol-notes.md:70:| Q01 | S1 | fixed | Line 66 and 67: RESERVE_MAH = B5 + 4.5 mAh, percent derived per ring size from the design capacity of architecture 17.17 (US 7 18.2 mAh: 29.73 % -> 30 %, 66.4 min above B5; US 13 24.7 mAh: 23.22 % -> 24 %, 68.5 min; all seven sizes at least 65.9 min); reported in `DEVICE_INFO` `reserve_pct` and `design_mah_x10` (line 505). Script `evidence/v03/calc03.py`, output `calc03.out` |
design/protocol/protocol-notes.md:99:| P01 | S1 | fixed | Section 2A, lines 60 to 87: reserve 25 % (line 64: 4.08 mAh above B5 = 61.2 min on 20.4 mAh), rolling budget 30 s screen, 1,000 ms motor, 120 s transfer (line 69), "Phone apps" setting and the user's exit in every mode (line 76), order of refusals (line 81). Codes 0x13 to 0x16 (lines 413 to 420); `GATE` lines in the check. Drain figures: `evidence/v02/budget_calc.out` (worst average 1.694 mA, 9.0 to 12.0 h from full to the reserve; review's 33.0 mA is 19 times it) |
design/protocol/evidence/broken/v02-f4.md:65:- Smallest cell 20.4 mAh (architecture 17.16). 25 % = 5.10 mAh; B5 (F4 section 1.1: 5 %) = 1.02 mAh; usable above B5 = 5.10 - 1.02 = 4.08 mAh = 4.08 / 4.0 h = 61.2 minutes [CALC]. 24 % would give 3.876 mAh = 58.1 minutes, under 60, so 25 % is the lowest whole percent that holds.
design/protocol/evidence/broken/v02-f4.md:74:- Total worst average 1.694 mA. On the smallest cell (20.4 mAh) that is 12.0 hours from full to empty, and 9.0 hours from full to the reserve (75 % of the cell); on 27.2 mAh, 16.1 and 12.0 hours; standby alone is 680 and 907 hours. The review-1 attack (33.0 mA) is 19 times the allowed average. A phone can still shorten standby from weeks to hours, but never below the reserve, and so never below 61 minutes of recording.
design/protocol/evidence/broken/v02-f4.md:150:Rules for switching: the ring requests FAST when the screen turns on in `STANDBY` or an error state, when it accepts `AUDIO_START` or `REC_FETCH`, and when it accepts `DISPLAY_COMMIT`; it requests LOW 2 s after the last of these ends; it sends at most one request per 2 s. **A request the phone rejects or ignores is repeated every 10 s; after 3 such requests in a row (30 s) with the link still faster than the LOW interval and no cause (no screen on, no stream, no download), the ring closes the link.** Reason: a link held at FAST for nothing costs 78.9 uA from the cell against 2.6 uA at LOW (section 14.3), 30 times more, and a ring that cannot lower it can only refuse the phone; the bound is also not an S1 risk, because 0.0789 mA continuous is 187 hours of the 20.4 mAh cell [CALC: 20.4 / (0.0789 + 0.03)]. The phone may connect again; the same rule applies to the new link. Everything in this protocol MUST work at the LOW set (slower, not broken). PHY and packet size: the ring requests the 2 Mbit/s PHY and a data length of 251 bytes after the connection is encrypted; if the phone refuses either, the protocol runs unchanged on 1 Mbit/s and 27 bytes per packet, only slower (**TO MEASURE**: which phones grant them). SOURCED reason for 2M and 251: Nordic's throughput sample measures 1,504 kbps with exactly these (nRF Connect SDK `samples/bluetooth/throughput/README.rst`, example log "LE PHY updated: TX PHY LE 2M", "LE data len updated: TX (len: 251 ...", "at 1504 kbps", evidence/nrf-throughput-readme.rst lines 224 to 231).
