design/protocol/evidence/broken/rev2/f1a_sensor_csv_only/ring-link-protocol.md:65:- Smallest cell 20.4 mAh (architecture 17.16). 25 % = 5.10 mAh; B5 (F4 section 1.1: 5 %) = 1.02 mAh; usable above B5 = 5.10 - 1.02 = 4.08 mAh = 4.08 / 4.0 h = 61.2 minutes [CALC]. 24 % would give 3.876 mAh = 58.1 minutes, under 60, so 25 % is the lowest whole percent that holds.
design/protocol/evidence/broken/rev2/f1a_sensor_csv_only/ring-link-protocol.md:74:- Total worst average 1.694 mA. On the smallest cell (20.4 mAh) that is 12.0 hours from full to empty, and 9.0 hours from full to the reserve (75 % of the cell); on 27.2 mAh, 16.1 and 12.0 hours; standby alone is 680 and 907 hours. The review-1 attack (33.0 mA) is 19 times the allowed average. A phone can still shorten standby from weeks to hours, but never below the reserve, and so never below 61 minutes of recording.
design/protocol/evidence/broken/rev2/f1a_sensor_csv_only/ring-link-protocol.md:150:Rules for switching: the ring requests FAST when the screen turns on in `STANDBY` or an error state, when it accepts `AUDIO_START` or `REC_FETCH`, and when it accepts `DISPLAY_COMMIT`; it requests LOW 2 s after the last of these ends; it sends at most one request per 2 s. **A request the phone rejects or ignores is repeated every 10 s; after 3 such requests in a row (30 s) with the link still faster than the LOW interval and no cause (no screen on, no stream, no download), the ring closes the link.** Reason: a link held at FAST for nothing costs 78.9 uA from the cell against 2.6 uA at LOW (section 14.3), 30 times more, and a ring that cannot lower it can only refuse the phone; the bound is also not an S1 risk, because 0.0789 mA continuous is 187 hours of the 20.4 mAh cell [CALC: 20.4 / (0.0789 + 0.03)]. The phone may connect again; the same rule applies to the new link. Everything in this protocol MUST work at the LOW set (slower, not broken). PHY and packet size: the ring requests the 2 Mbit/s PHY and a data length of 251 bytes after the connection is encrypted; if the phone refuses either, the protocol runs unchanged on 1 Mbit/s and 27 bytes per packet, only slower (**TO MEASURE**: which phones grant them). SOURCED reason for 2M and 251: Nordic's throughput sample measures 1,504 kbps with exactly these (nRF Connect SDK `samples/bluetooth/throughput/README.rst`, example log "LE PHY updated: TX PHY LE 2M", "LE data len updated: TX (len: 251 ...", "at 1504 kbps", evidence/nrf-throughput-readme.rst lines 224 to 231).
